# github-pr

Own every GitHub PR conversation posted on the user's behalf, from either side. The modes:

- `review ready` discovers review requests and dispatches one interactive reviewer per PR.
- `review <n>` dispatches that PR when necessary; inside its recorded worktree, it conducts the review, triages findings with the user, and posts only after confirmation.
- `respond <n>` drafts the author's replies to the PR's unanswered review comments, walks the user through each, and posts only after confirmation.

A bare `<n>`, or no mode at all, does not pick one. Ask whether to review or respond, suggesting `respond` when the authenticated user is the PR's author and `review` otherwise.

This skill may be invoked directly, by any agent, or repeatedly with `/loop 5m /github-pr review ready` in a Claude session that remains open. Both GitHub scripts below, and the schema of everything posted, are in [references/posting.md](references/posting.md); read it before reading a PR's threads or posting.

## Open with the summary

A `review <n>` session conducting its review, and every `respond <n>` session, opens with the PR's summary. Several of these sessions run at once, and the summary is how the user tells which PR a pane is about.

Before reading anything else about the PR, run the `summarize-github-pr` skill. Your first message to the user is its output, printed verbatim as markdown, before any status line or other text; the script's output reaches only you, so the user sees the summary only when you print it. Do not rebuild, reword, or add to it, except to note workflow status that has gone wrong, such as a checkout that doesn't match.

## `review ready`: dispatch the queue

This mode requires Herdr (`HERDR_ENV=1`). Read [references/queue.md](references/queue.md), then run the queue and dispatch flow there. Do not ask the user to confirm the discovered set: `review ready` is explicit authorization to launch the requested review queue, including when a loop invoked it.

Each PR uses a local branch named exactly as GitHub's `headRefName`, because Claude uses that identity to link the checkout to its PR. Never substitute a numbered or suffixed branch. A conflicting unrelated local branch is skipped and reported without modification.

The queue mode removes finished review worktrees, then dispatches or refreshes reviewers. A worktree is removed once its pass is complete, whatever the review's outcome; if the PR needs another look, a new review request brings it back in a fresh worktree. It does not wait for reviewers, collect their findings, triage, or post.

## `review <n>`: dispatch or conduct the review

Resolve the PR. If the current checkout is not its recorded exact-head-branch review worktree, read [references/queue.md](references/queue.md) and run that document's prepare, create, mark, and launch flow for this PR alone, then stop; the launched reviewer continues interactively in its pane. If the current checkout is the recorded worktree for this PR and head SHA, conduct the review here.

Read the PR title, author, body, base branch, current head SHA, and changed files, and run `scripts/feedback.py <n>` for my prior reviews and the threads I started. On a re-review, include unresolved prior findings in the review brief so the reviewer verifies them against the current code and does not duplicate findings that still stand.

Invoke the shared `review` skill once with a self-contained brief containing:

- the PR's stated intent and base branch
- the change scope (`<base>...HEAD` in this checkout)
- relevant unresolved prior findings and their thread comment IDs
- an explicit instruction not to report Nits

While the reviewer runs, tell the user in a line or two that it is running and what you asked it to focus on. The summary that opened the session already describes the PR.

The shared reviewer returns Blockers and Follow-ups without editing, interaction, a verdict, or posting. Preserve its classifications unless discussion with the user supplies new technical evidence.

On a re-review, each prior finding the reviewer verifies as fixed is a thread to resolve: resolving is how the reviewer confirms a fix holds. A prior finding that still stands gets a reply on its thread instead of a new inline comment.

Triage follows [Triage with the user](#triage-with-the-user), with the review's findings as its items; when the review found nothing, go straight to the confirmation. Then derive the GitHub event from the included findings: any Blocker means `REQUEST_CHANGES`; otherwise use `APPROVE`. A Follow-up never changes the event by itself. If no findings remain, the review is an approval. The final confirmation shows the event, the included findings, and the threads to be resolved.

Render the confirmed review into a batch with one inline comment per included finding where possible, replies on the threads of still-open prior findings, and `resolve` for the verified fixes. Prefix each finding body with `[Blocker]` or `[Follow-up]`.

When the workflow reaches a terminal decision (successfully posted, or the user explicitly decides to finish without posting), record the pass complete with `scripts/dispatch.py complete`. Leave it active if posting failed or the user has not finished triage, so a changed PR head is deferred rather than replacing the checkout underneath an unfinished review.

## `respond <n>`: reply as the author

Run this inline, in the session that invokes it; it dispatches nothing. Work from a checkout of the PR's head when one exists, so answers rest on the code as it stands. If the authenticated user is not the PR's author, say so and ask before going on.

Run `scripts/feedback.py <n>` and take every `unanswered` inline thread, whoever started it, and every review summary body and conversation comment that is not my own. Whether a summary or comment was already answered is a judgment the API can't make: list those after `my_latest` as needing replies, and the older ones separately as possibly answered, so triage settles them rather than a timestamp. Bot reviewers are included; say which items are a bot's, so the user can skip them quickly.

Draft one reply for each item, grounded in evidence rather than intent:

- **Fixed**: name the commit that fixes it and what changed, such as `Fixed in 4f1c2e9: the token now expires after an hour.` The commit must already be on the PR; cite it only after reading its diff against the comment.
- **Declined**: the reason, stated plainly and without arguing.
- **Answered**: for a question, the answer from the code.
- **Deferred**: what will happen instead, such as a follow-up ticket, when the caller or the user has said so.

When nothing settles an item (no commit addresses it and no one has said whether it will be fixed), don't invent a stance. Show the user the comment, mark the reply undecided, and let triage settle it.

Triage follows [Triage with the user](#triage-with-the-user), with the drafts as its items: show each reviewer's comment and its draft reply, and `Other` edits the reply. The final confirmation shows every reply that will post and where it lands.

Render the confirmed replies into a batch with no review: `thread_replies` for inline threads, and at most one `issue_comment` that answers review summary bodies and conversation comments by naming or quoting each point it addresses. List every commit a reply cites in `commits`. Never resolve a thread in this mode, even one whose fix has landed: that confirmation is the reviewer's to give.

## Triage with the user

Open triage with a one-line count of the items, such as `2 Blockers and 3 Follow-ups`, then raise ✋🏽 and ask the first item's question.

Walk through every item individually. Ask only whether that item should be included; do not ask for an overall verdict yet.

- With a structured question UI, offer `Include`, `Exclude`, and `Other`. `Other` opens discussion or lets the user rewrite or reclassify the item.
- Without one, ask: `Should this be included?` A bare yes includes it, a bare no excludes it, and a fuller answer opens discussion.

Keep the question concise: what the item is and its concrete consequence. Do not put the full technical body or file coordinates in the question unless the user asks to discuss it. Preserve each ruling as you proceed.

After triage, show everything that will post and explicitly ask for confirmation before posting. Open the confirmation with the summary's identity table (its first table, number and title over author and URL) copied from the opening summary, so the user can see which PR it is posting to. If the user declines, revise or stop as directed. Never infer posting approval from the earlier per-item rulings.

## Post

Render the confirmed batch with this exact header, so every posted message is attributed:

```markdown
{{ includeTemplate ".chezmoitemplates/posting-attribution.md" . | trim }}
```

Set `head_sha` to the head the content was formed against. Run `scripts/post.py --dry-run batch.json` and resolve every problem it reports. If the PR's head has moved, do not post content formed against the stale code: tell the user a new pass is needed and, in a review pass, leave it active so `review ready` reports the stale head rather than changing the checkout underneath the current session. Only then run `scripts/post.py batch.json`. If a step fails after part of the batch landed, retry only with the remainder batch the poster prints, never the original. Do not fall back to hand-built `gh` posting if the script fails. Posting is the only external mutation this skill performs, and only after the confirmation above.

## Signals

Several of these sessions often run at once, and the user moves between them from the Herdr sidebar, so a `review <n>` or `respond <n>` session that is not working always shows a signal. Use the `signal-user` skill as the last step of every turn, not only at the stops below: the user's reply takes the last ✋🏽 or ✓ down, so a turn spent on an aside or a follow-up question that ends without one leaves the sidebar blank. Pick the first that holds:

- **✋🏽 when anything waits on the user**: a review ready to triage (`#254 ready to triage`) or replies drafted (`#254 replies drafted`), each item question, the posting confirmation (`#254 confirm post`), a head that moved so a new pass is needed, or any other question. Raise it before asking, since a question prompt holds the turn until the user answers.
- **✓ when the pass is over**: the batch posted (`#254 posted`), or the user chose to finish without posting (`#254 done, not posted`). It stays the answer on every later turn, until something new waits on the user.
- **… when nothing waits on the user** and the session ends its turn while something it started will wake it, such as a review running in the background (`#254 reviewing`).

The `review ready` queue mode itself signals nothing; it dispatches and stops, and each reviewer it launches signals for itself.

## Boundaries

- Do not edit the PR's source in any mode.
- Do not request or report Nits.
- Do not launch more than one active review pass for the same PR head.
- Do not update an active review worktree when the PR head changes; report it as stale and let a later `review ready` run refresh it after the pass completes.
- Do not remove a review worktree except through `review ready`'s cleanup step, or when the user separately asks.
- Resolve only threads I started, and only in a review.
